    {
      "content": "Facts: Margaret reported that Vale’s Performance in 3Q24 report, dated October 24, 2024, covers the quarter ended September 30, 2024, and gives amounts in US$ millions. Her reported free-cash-flow bridge starts with pro forma adjusted EBITDA—not ordinary adjusted EBITDA—of 3,741, then subtracts working capital (570), Brumadinho and dam de-characterization expenses (420), income taxes and REFIS (471), capital expenditures (1,398), associates and joint ventures (242), and other items (461), arriving at free cash flow of 179. The arithmetic reconciles: 3,741 − 570 − 420 − 471 − 1,398 − 242 − 461 = 179. She said the 420 includes 342 of disbursements related to previously provisioned Brumadinho and dam de-characterization expenses and 78 of incurred expenses. The 461 other-items line includes interest, derivatives, leasing, Samarco-related payments, and other items. The report’s footnote describes the 242 adjustment as relating to associates and JV EBITDA included in pro forma EBITDA.\n\nMargaret also said that, after the 179 free-cash-flow figure, the report shows a separate 2,121 outflow for cash management and others, resulting in a 1,942 decrease in cash and equivalents. She said that later line includes items such as shareholder distributions and debt payments, partly offset by new borrowing and proceeds from the VODC sale; it is not another deduction in the calculation of reported free cash flow.\n\nFor Q3 2024 costs, Margaret said the report labels 55.1/t as iron ore fines and pellets all-in costs, or cash-cost break-even landed in China. The described cost stack is C1 cash cost including third-party purchases, 24.0/t; freight, 20.6/t; distribution, 2.5/t; expenses and royalties, 5.4/t; moisture adjustment, 4.3/t; and fines quality adjustment, 1.9/t, giving fines all-in cost of 58.7/t. Subtracting the pellet business contribution of 3.6/t gives 55.1/t. The 63.3/t figure is the 55.1/t all-in cost plus 8.2/t of sustaining investment for fines and pellets. The separate 20.6/t C1 cash cost excluding third-party purchases is an FOB measure; Margaret said the report footnote describes it as ex-ROM and ex-royalties, and it does not include freight. The report also lists freight at 20.6/t as a separate cost line. These are all Q3 2024 figures, not current cost data.",
