    {
      "content": "Facts: The figures Margaret supplied are for Q3 2024, the quarter ended September 30, 2024, and are historical rather than current. Her attributed free-cash-flow bridge, in US$ millions, starts with pro forma adjusted EBITDA—not ordinary adjusted EBITDA—of 3,741 and deducts working capital (570), Brumadinho and dam de-characterization expenses (420), income taxes and REFIS (471), capital expenditures (1,398), associates and joint ventures (242), and other items (461), to reach reported free cash flow of 179. The figures reconcile arithmetically. The US$420 million includes US$342 million of disbursements for previously provisioned Brumadinho and dam de-characterization expenses and US$78 million of incurred expenses. The report’s footnote describes the US$242 million adjustment as relating to associates and JV EBITDA included in pro forma EBITDA. The US$461 million other-items line includes interest, derivatives, leasing, Samarco-related payments, and other items. After free cash flow, the report shows a further US$2.121 billion outflow for cash management and others, resulting in a US$1.942 billion decrease in cash and equivalents. That later line includes items such as shareholder distributions and debt payments, partly offset by new borrowing and proceeds from the VODC sale; it is not a deduction in the US$179 million free-cash-flow bridge.\n\nFor Q3 2024 costs, the report labels US$55.1/t as iron ore fines and pellets all-in costs (cash cost break-even landed in China). Its cost stack includes C1 cash cost including third-party purchases (US$24.0/t), freight (US$20.6/t), distribution (US$2.5/t), expenses and royalties (US$5.4/t), moisture adjustment (US$4.3/t), and fines quality adjustment (US$1.9/t), producing fines all-in cost of US$58.7/t. Subtracting the pellet business contribution of US$3.6/t yields US$55.1/t. Freight is included in that China-landed figure. The US$63.3/t figure adds US$8.2/t of sustaining investment to the US$55.1/t all-in cost. The separate US$20.6/t C1 cash cost excluding third-party purchases is an FOB measure; the report’s footnote says it is ex-ROM and ex-royalties, and it does not include freight. The separately listed US$20.6/t freight line happens to have the same amount but is a different cost item and scope. None of these Q3 2024 cost figures is current data.\n\nThe general cash-flow principles Eleanor described do not establish Vale-specific accounting definitions. Operating cash flow less cash capex may be a useful rough measure, but it cannot be assumed to reconcile to Vale’s reported free cash flow. Current iron-ore demand, current Vale financial position, current market quotation, and current valuation remain unverified. The identified 2024 materials do not support treating any of these as current or drawing a current company-level investment conclusion. Portfolio suitability also remains unresolved and requires actual household and portfolio details.",
